[stderr] -## Provenance, review, correction, and withdrawal lifecycle
[stderr] +## GPI-007 — reproducible editorial lifecycle
[stderr]  
[stderr] -Each state-changing event is append-only and has an event ID, card ID,
[stderr] -from-state, to-state, revision, UTC timestamp, actor role, reason code, and
[stderr] -the evidence references used. Replaying events in `(timestamp, event ID)` order
[stderr] -must recreate the catalogue state; an event whose `from-state` does not equal
[stderr] -the replayed state is invalid. The release selector only reads `published`.
[stderr] +A transition input is `(cardId, currentRevision, event, evidenceRefs,
[stderr] +actorRole, occurredAt)`. It emits an append-only event and either next state or
[stderr] +validation failure. Time is recorded UTC and never supplies a missing
[stderr] +transition. A revision is immutable after leaving `draft`; correction creates
[stderr] +a successor revision under the same stable ID.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -| From | Event / required evidence | To | Eligibility |
[stderr] +| Current state | Permitted event / required evidence | Next state | Eligible |
[stderr]  | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] -| `draft` | `submit_for_fact_check`; complete provenance and assertion list | `fact_checked` | No |
[stderr] -| `fact_checked` | `approve_accessibility_copy`; distinct second reviewer and reviewed revision | `second_reviewed` | No |
[stderr] -| `second_reviewed` | `publish`; release-editor approval and validation pass | `published` | Yes |
[stderr] -| `draft`, `fact_checked`, `second_reviewed` | `block`; ambiguity, source/rights uncertainty, or review rejection reason | `blocked` | No |
[stderr] -| `published` | `withdraw`; contested fact, correction, rights issue, or failed post-publication review; effective timestamp and reason | `withdrawn` | No, immediately for new selections |
[stderr] -| `blocked`, `withdrawn` | `revise`; new positive revision linked to prior revision and corrected evidence | `draft` | No |
[stderr] -| `withdrawn` | `retain_historical_reference`; no state change, only a round-version link | `withdrawn` | No |
[stderr] +| `draft` | `submit_factual_check`: provenance, originality, accepted factual review | `fact_checked` | no |
[stderr] +| `fact_checked` | `accept_accessibility_copy`: different reviewer, accepted revision review | `accessibility_edited` | no |
[stderr] +| `accessibility_edited` | `accept_second_review`: distinct reviewer, grammar/alias validation | `second_reviewed` | no |
[stderr] +| `second_reviewed` | `publish`: release editor, all release checks pass | `published` | yes |
[stderr] +| pre-publication | `block`: dispute, missing rights, or validation failure | `blocked` | no |
[stderr] +| `published` | `withdraw`: reason, effective UTC time, evidence | `withdrawn` | no |
[stderr] +| `blocked` or `withdrawn` | `revise`: creates r+1 and preserves history/correction note | `draft` (r+1) | no |
[stderr]  
[stderr] -Withdrawal never rewrites a completed round: historical results keep the
[stderr] -original card revision and explanation. A withdrawal may name a replacement
[stderr] -card, but a replacement is independently reviewed and published; it is never
[stderr] -automatically substituted into an active or historical run. A contested fact
[stderr] -is withdrawn first, then revised only after source review resolves it. This
[stderr] -prevents a selector from guessing between versions or facts.
[stderr] +No other transition is allowed: no direct draft-to-published, self-review, or
[stderr] +silent reopen. Each event records from-state, to-state, card/revision, event,
[stderr] +reason/evidence, actor ID/role, and UTC time. Replay from draft reproduces
[stderr] +state; invalid events remain rejected audit entries and change no state.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +A reported conflict blocks an unpublished card or withdraws a published card
[stderr] +immediately. Withdrawal records `factual_correction`, `source_rights`,
[stderr] +`ambiguity`, or `accessibility_defect`, effective time, evidence, and an
[stderr] +optional replacement. It does not silently replace cards or rewrite past rounds.
[stderr] +New selection excludes it from that time; a presented attempt resolves against
[stderr] +its presented revision. Only a corrected, newly reviewed revision may publish.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Seeded anti-repeat and shortage policy
[stderr] +
[stderr] +For `(catalogueSnapshotId, seed, requestedCount, filter, ledger)`, freeze the
[stderr] +published eligible catalogue at the snapshot, apply filter, then remove
[stderr] +blocked/withdrawn cards. `recent` is the union of IDs in the two latest
[stderr] +*completed* rounds, sorting rounds by `(completedAtUtc, roundId)` descending.
[stderr] +Draft, abandoned, and in-progress rounds do not count.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -## Seeded anti-repeat and shortage policy (`SEL-1`)
[stderr] +Set `fresh = candidates − recent`. Assign each candidate
[stderr] +`SHA-256(seed + "|" + catalogueSnapshotId + "|" + cardId)`, then sort
[stderr] +ascending by `(key, cardId)`. Take the first
[stderr] +`min(requestedCount, fresh.length)`; fill only the remainder from
[stderr] +`reuse = candidates ∩ recent` in the same order. Whenever reuse occurs or
[stderr] +eligible cards are too few, record:
[stderr]  
[stderr] -At round creation, take an immutable eligibility snapshot: current batch
[stderr] -revision, selected theme, all `published` and non-withdrawn cards satisfying
[stderr] -the requested category, and the completed-round ledger. The ledger supplies
[stderr] -the card sets from the two most recent *completed* rounds in the same profile;
[stderr] -abandoned or in-progress rounds do not count.
[stderr] +`{ requestedCount, freshAvailable, eligibleAvailable, reusedCardIds,
[stderr] +unfilledCount, disclosureKey: "selection_shortage_reuse_disclosed" }`.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -1. Exclude every snapshot card seen in either ledger set **when at least the
[stderr] -   requested number of other eligible cards remains**.
[stderr] -2. If that exclusion leaves fewer cards than requested, retain the full
[stderr] -   eligible pool and set `reuseDisclosure = true`; the round explicitly says
[stderr] -   that familiar cards may appear because the theme has too few alternatives.
[stderr] -3. Rank the selected pool by ascending `rankToken` from the recorded fixture
[stderr] -   seed. Tie-break with ascending stable card ID. Take the first requested
[stderr] -   number, then store the eligibility snapshot, seed, rank tokens, ledger
[stderr] -   round IDs, selection, and disclosure flag in the round record.
[stderr] +Show this plain-language disclosure before round start whenever reuse or an
[stderr] +unfilled count exists. No clock, random source, network response, unpublished
[stderr] +card, or iteration order affects selection. Equal full input has equal IDs and
[stderr] +shortage record.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -Production derives a rank token from a versioned deterministic hash of
[stderr] -`SEL-1 | seed | batch revision | card ID`; the exact hash implementation is
[stderr] -fixed by the core rules release and must preserve the stored token. The fixtures
[stderr] -below provide the acceptance vectors without relying on random runtime state.
[stderr] +### Eight seeded selection fixtures
[stderr]  
[stderr] -| Fixture | Requested / eligible snapshot (rank order) | Two latest completed ledger sets | Expected selection | Disclosure |
[stderr] -| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
