[stderr] +# Adjudication and freshness bible
[stderr]  
[stderr] -**Owns:** `GAME-003`, `GAME-004`, `GPI-006`, and `GPI-007`.  This is a
[stderr] -release-one rules record, not an implementation specification. It applies to
[stderr] -published revisions in the bounded QB-A catalogue and the tutorial cards.
[stderr] -It does not permit fuzzy matching, live facts, or inference from player input.
[stderr] +**Scope:** accepted evidence for `GAME-003`, `GAME-004`, `GPI-006`, and
[stderr] +`GPI-007`. This is a deterministic future-core contract, not implementation or
[stderr] +published content. A result is calculated only from stated input; it is never
[stderr] +inferred or guessed.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -## Deterministic adjudication contract
[stderr] +## GPI-006 — answer adjudication
[stderr]  
[stderr] -An attempt is evaluated against the card revision embedded in its round. The
[stderr] -evaluator returns exactly one of `correct`, `incorrect`, or `invalid_input`;
[stderr] -it does not mutate the card, add aliases, or select a likely answer. A resolved
[stderr] -attempt records `cardId`, `revision`, `ruleVersion` (`ADJ-1`), submitted value,
[stderr] -outcome, canonical display answer, and explanation key. Re-submitting the same
[stderr] -record produces the same outcome.
[stderr] +Every resolved attempt is immutable and records `attemptId`, `cardId`,
[stderr] +`cardRevision`, `format`, submitted value, `outcome`, `reasonCode`,
[stderr] +canonical answer, and explanation key. Feedback reopening cannot recompute
[stderr] +against a later revision. A card withdrawn after presentation may resolve that
[stderr] +presented attempt; it cannot enter a new selection.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -| Format | Permitted submitted value | Deterministic rule | Never permitted |
[stderr] +| Format | Comparison | Accept only when | Reject examples |
[stderr]  | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] -| `four_option_select` | One configured option ID | Correct only when the ID equals `canonicalAnswer.optionId`. | Comparing option label text, position, or a partial ID. |
[stderr] -| `true_false` | Canonical ID `true` or `false` | Correct only when it equals `canonicalAnswer.booleanId`. | Treating `yes`, `no`, `1`, or `0` as an answer. |
[stderr] -| `ordered_pair` | Exactly two configured IDs, in sequence | Correct only when `[firstId, secondId]` exactly equals the configured ordered pair. | Reordering, set comparison, or accepting display text. |
[stderr] -| `short_typed_answer` | A string | Correct only when its normalised value equals the normalised canonical answer or one configured accepted alias. | Edit distance, phonetics, autocomplete, external lookup, or a newly learned alias. |
[stderr] +| `four_option_select` | Exact option ID | It equals the one correct option ID. | `blank`, `unknown_option_id`, `incorrect_option` |
[stderr] +| `true_false` | Exact canonical ID | It is exact `true` or `false` and correct. | `blank`, `malformed_boolean_id`, `incorrect_option` |
[stderr] +| `ordered_pair` | Exact two-item ordered ID array | It has two known IDs in configured order, including intentional repeats. | `blank`, `wrong_arity`, `unknown_option_id`, `wrong_order` |
[stderr] +| `short_typed_answer` | Normalised text vs. canonical display answer plus aliases | It equals one unique configured normalised value. | `blank`, `normalises_empty`, `unconfigured_variant`, `ambiguous_alias` |
[stderr]  
[stderr] -Missing, wrong-type, unconfigured-option, and blank values are `invalid_input`
[stderr] -until the player explicitly confirms a blank response. A confirmed blank is an
[stderr] -`incorrect` attempt. Every incorrect answer receives the card revision's
[stderr] -canonical answer and explanation; it does not assert why a near match was
[stderr] -rejected.
[stderr] +A rejected submission always exposes canonical answer and explanation. A
[stderr] +configuration error (including duplicate normalised alias) makes the card
[stderr] +ineligible; it never accepts, guesses, or silently maps an answer.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### Typed-answer normalisation and aliases
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Apply these steps, in order, to submitted text and every authored canonical
[stderr] +answer/alias when validating a card revision:
[stderr]  
[stderr] -### Typed-answer normalisation and alias policy (`ADJ-1`)
[stderr] +1. Convert input to Unicode NFC.
[stderr] +2. Apply Unicode default case folding (locale-independent).
[stderr] +3. Replace each Unicode whitespace sequence with one ASCII space and trim.
[stderr] +4. Decompose to Unicode NFD, remove combining marks, and recompose to NFC.
[stderr] +5. Remove only Unicode punctuation. Letters, numbers, and symbols remain; no
[stderr] +   transliteration, word substitution, reordering, stemming, phonetics,
[stderr] +   edit-distance threshold, AI, or fuzzy matching occurs.
[stderr] +6. Collapse whitespace again and trim. An empty key rejects as
[stderr] +   `normalises_empty`.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -Normalisation is a pure function, applied to both a submitted typed string and
[stderr] -every configured canonical/alias string, in this exact order:
[stderr] +The key is comparison-only. An alias is accepted only if deliberately authored
[stderr] +for that revision, non-empty after normalisation, and unique in the card answer
[stderr] +domain. Validator failure rejects aliases colliding with another answer, another
[stderr] +alias/distractor, or multiple possible answers. Any canonical wording, alias, or
[stderr] +normalisation-version change creates a new revision, review, and validation; it
[stderr] +cannot change a resolved attempt.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -1. Reject non-string input; Unicode-normalise with NFKD, then Unicode casefold.
[stderr] -2. Replace the explicitly mapped letters `ł→l`, `đ→d`, `ð→d`, `þ→th`,
[stderr] -   `æ→ae`, and `œ→oe`; remove all remaining combining marks.
[stderr] -3. Replace every Unicode punctuation or separator character with one ASCII
[stderr] -   space; retain letters and decimal digits only. Do not transliterate any
[stderr] -   other character or translate words.
[stderr] -4. Collapse adjacent spaces and trim. The resulting empty string is blank.
[stderr] +Thus `Jürgen Klopp`, `JURGEN   KLOPP`, and `Jürgen-Klopp` share
[stderr] +`jurgen klopp`; `PSG` matches Paris Saint-Germain only if authored as an
[stderr] +alias. `M. Salah`, `Salahh`, or a missing first name never matches unless
[stderr] +explicitly configured and reviewed.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -An alias is author-configured, localised, and revision-scoped. It must be a
[stderr] -non-blank string whose normalised value differs from no other canonical answer
[stderr] -or alias in the eligible scope of that card. The validator rejects duplicate
[stderr] -normalised values, aliases that name another option/answer, and aliases added
[stderr] -after publication without a new reviewed revision. Display spelling is never
[stderr] -changed by normalisation. This deliberate finite list makes acceptance
[stderr] -explainable: an unlisted spelling is rejected even if a person might infer it.
